Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Custom "Cancel" Back Button (in your XAML)

```xml
<!-- EditProductPage.xaml -->
<ContentPage ...>
    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                           Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 2. Navigation Guard (in AppShell.xaml.cs)

Override `OnNavigating` to intercept back navigation and show a confirmation dialog:

```csharp
// AppShell.xaml.cs
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    if (args.Source == ShellNavigationSource.Pop &&
        Shell.Current.CurrentPage is EditProductPage page &&
        page.BindingContext is EditProductViewModel vm &&
        vm.HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        var deferral = args.GetDeferral();

        bool discard = await Shell.Current.CurrentPage.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard them?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();

        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

## 3. ViewModel CancelCommand

```csharp
// EditProductViewModel.cs
public bool HasUnsavedChanges { get; set; }

[RelayCommand]
private async Task Cancel()
{
    // This triggers OnNavigating above, which handles the confirmation
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

**Key points:**
- `GetDeferral()` is required for async checks — without it the navigation completes before your dialog returns.
- `args.Cancel()` stops navigation if the user chooses "Stay".
- The `CancelCommand` uses `GoToAsync("..")` which fires as a `Pop` source, so the same guard catches both the custom button and system back gestures.
